    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can u go a bit deeper into the cellular level stuff? like what actually happens inside the cell when that signal hits? i'm trying to explain it to a client so i need to be really specific about the biochemical steps.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When leucine is abundant in the cellular environment, it interacts with the Sestrin2 protein and triggers an mTORC1 signaling cascade. This starts with leucine binding to a leucyl-proline-rich element (LPR) region on Sestrin2, activating it. Activated Sestrin2 interacts with other proteins in the complex to initiate the mTORC1 pathway.\n\nOnce stimulated by Sestrin2, mTORC1 phosphorylates downstream targets such as ribosomal protein S6 kinase (p70S6K) and the eukaryotic initiation factor 4E-binding protein (4E-BP1). Phosphorylation of these factors releases the initiation of cap-dependent mRNA translation. Consequently, ribosomes assemble around the mRNA and begin synthesizing proteins at a considerably increased rate. This series of interactions amplifies the cell's ability to synthesize proteins in response to increased leucine availability, contributing to muscle hypertrophy.\n\nAdditionally, Sestrin2-dependent activation of mTORC1 leads to the inhibition of 4E-BP1, which further promotes protein synthesis by allowing mRNA to be accessible for translation. The role of Sestrin2 extends further as it is also involved in the",
      "source": "target",
